# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-autocad-hung-instance-blocks-com-bootstrap — a stuck acad.exe the sweep cannot end refuses every later COM session
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

verify:epure-api:windows on master (alfe 2.2.96, job 16753534728,
2026-09-26 11:30). Both AutoCAD runs died in BOOTSTRAP:

#+begin_example
alfe: alfe AUTOCAD backend error (phase BOOTSTRAP): AutoCAD COM bridge
  (cscript) exited before READY (exit 0):
  ...\bridge-autocad.vbs(189, 1) (null):
  L'appel a ete rejete par l'appele. [PROCESS-EXITED-BEFORE-READY]
#+end_example

"L'appel a ete rejete par l'appele" is =RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED= (0x80010001):
the COM server refused the call. The cause is in the same trace, in the
sweep that runs before every Windows CAD job:

#+begin_example
sweep: 97 instance(s) recorded
sweep: ending orphaned acad.exe pid 21928 (created 20260925132249...)
sweep: pid 21928 survived Stop-Process
sweep: ending orphaned acad.exe pid 17640 (created 20260925140116...)
sweep: pid 17640 survived Stop-Process
   ... (97 "survived Stop-Process" lines) ...
sweep: 0 ended, 97 left
#+end_example

Two acad.exe from the PREVIOUS DAY (13:22 and 14:01 on 2026-09-25) are
still running and survive Stop-Process every time. A hung AutoCAD owns
the =AutoCAD.Application= COM registration, and it is that instance
=GetObject=/=CreateObject= reaches -- so it answers RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED
(it is modal, or wedged) and every subsequent AutoCAD session fails
before READY, whatever the session asks for.

So this masks everything else on the AutoCAD side: the EPURE API
question cannot be answered on AutoCAD while those two processes exist.

* Two defects, and they compound

1. *The sweep cannot end a hung instance.* =scripts/sweep-orphaned-cad.ps1=
   uses =Stop-Process -Force=, reports "survived", and proceeds. It also
   reports each of the 97 recorded entries separately although they are
   two distinct PIDs -- the created-CAD registry accumulates an entry per
   session and is never pruned, so the log is 97 lines for 2 processes.
2. *The bridge treats RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED as fatal.* A rejected call is
   the documented "server is busy" answer in AutoCAD automation, and the
   standard handling is to retry with a short back-off (that is what an
   =IMessageFilter= does for a native client). Failing the bootstrap on
   the first rejection makes a transiently busy AutoCAD indistinguishable
   from a wedged one.

* Directions

- Escalate in the sweep: after Stop-Process fails, try
  =taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F=; if the process still exists, FAIL THE JOB
  EARLY with a message naming the PIDs, instead of letting a doomed
  AutoCAD bootstrap run and report a COM error that looks like alfe's
  fault. A hung CAD is a runner-hygiene problem and must be reported as
  one.
- Prune the created-CAD registry: drop entries whose PID no longer
  exists, so the log reflects reality and the sweep does not re-report
  the same two processes 97 times.
- Retry RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED in bridge-autocad.vbs (a few attempts, ~1 s
  apart) before giving up, and say in the final message how many
  attempts were made.
- Then re-run verify:epure-api:windows to get the AutoCAD half of the
  EPURE API answer (alfe-plugin-epure-windows-validation).

* Note

The same job answered the BricsCAD half: the file run reached READY in
25.7 s and reported =DONE 1 FAIL= with exit 1, which is the 2.2.91 fix
working on a real CAD -- before it, that run was a silent success.

* Progress — clautolisp 2.2.102: all three directions implemented, NONE
  verified on the machine

Both defects named above are addressed, and the third direction (pruning
the registry) with them. What cannot be done from here is the
verification: that runner's AutoCAD is the thing that does not start, and
the machine was asleep for this work. Everything below is therefore
written and unit-tested, and still has to be seen working on PF5S26BT.

*The sweep escalates and then refuses* (=scripts/sweep-orphaned-cad.ps1=):

- =taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F= after =Stop-Process= fails -- the stronger
  hammer, and it takes the process's children with it;
- if the process is STILL there, the sweep FAILS THE JOB (exit 1) naming
  each surviving PID and its creation stamp, and saying that they hold the
  COM registration so an AutoCAD session would only report a rejected
  call. A doomed bootstrap no longer runs and no longer looks like an alfe
  defect. =CAD_SWEEP_IGNORE_SURVIVORS=1= is the escape hatch for a job
  that wants to proceed anyway;
- the recorded entries are DEDUPLICATED on PID + creation stamp, and the
  count of raw lines is printed beside the count of instances. That is the
  97-lines-for-2-processes noise: the registry gains a line per session
  and was replayed in full.

*The bridge retries a rejected call* (the emitted bridge-autocad.vbs):
=TouchApp= wraps the FIRST call on the app object (=app.Visible = True=,
which is where the rejection landed) in ten attempts a second apart,
because RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED means BUSY -- a modal dialog, a load in
progress -- and a native client would have an IMessageFilter retry for it.
The give-up message says how many attempts were made and that another
instance is probably wedged.

Test: =autocad-bridge-retries-a-rejected-com-call= pins the helper, the
guarded first touch, the absence of the bare =app.Visible = True=, the
attempt count in the message, and that the file is still ASCII-only
VBScript that cscript can parse. alfe suite 1189 checks, 0 failures.

** What remains

- *Verification on the runner.* Play =verify:autocad-activex:windows= (or
  any AutoCAD job) once the machine is awake: the sweep should now either
  end the two acad.exe from 2026-09-25 or fail the job naming them, and a
  transiently busy AutoCAD should be waited out rather than aborted.
- *The two wedged processes themselves* (pids 17640 and 21928, created
  2026-09-25 13:22 and 14:01) may well need ending by hand once, since
  they predate the escalation.
